 "substitutedBy": null,
 "summary": "Establishes an interagency council for the purpose of coordinated planning on issues related to older adults which shall assess current programs and services across multiple agencies and shall consider current and projected needs.",
 "title": "Establishes an interagency council for the purpose of coordinated planning on issues related to older adults",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "S2847",
     "basePrintNoStr": "S2847-2017",
     "printNo": "S2847",
     "session": 2017,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": "\n \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 187\n \nTO THE SENATE:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nSenate Bill Number 2847, entitled:\n \n    \"AN ACT to establish an interagency council for the purpose of coor-\n      dinated planning on issues related to older adults\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  This  bill  would  establish an interagency council of representatives\nfrom 11 executive agencies to develop a statewide plan  to  address  the\ncurrent and future needs of older adults. Under the direction of the New\nYork  State Office for the Aging (NYSOFA), the Council would be required\nto assess current programs and services  across  multiple  agencies.  It\nwould  then  have  to complete a prospective 5-year report to ensure the\nfuture needs of older adults are being addressed.\n\n  This bill is identical to a bill I vetoed last year, and as  such,  it\nsuffers  from  the  same  flaws. (Veto No. 237 of 2016). Not only is the\nbill duplicative of federal  requirements  for  which  NYSOFA  is  fully\ncompliant,  but  it also fails to provide a funding source to ensure the\nbill would be implemented effectively.\n \n  First, the Federal Older Americans Act requires all states to  develop\na  comprehensive  5-year plan that describes how services will be organ-\nized and delivered. NYSOFA is fully compliant, as it submitted and post-\ned its most recent 5-year plan in 2015. Notably, while NYSOFA is charged\nwith the development of this plan, it sought broad community input  from\nthe Area Agencies on Aging, consumers, service providers, and educators.\n \n  Second, requiring NYSOFA to coordinate a new interagency council would\nput an unnecessary and unbudgeted strain on State resources. Substantial\nfunds  are  already allocated to produce the 5-year statewide report, as\nwell as for NYSOFA to chair or participate in several established inter-\nagency councils. For example, NYSOFA already:\n \n  Chairs an interagency task  force  that  includes  the  Department  of\nHealth  (DOH),  the  Office  of  People  with Developmental Disabilities\n(OPWDD), the Office of Mental Health (OMH), and the  Office  of  Alcohol\nand  Substance Abuse Services (OASAS) to design and implement a No Wrong\nDoor approach to accessing long term services.\n \n  Co-Chairs the State's Geriatric Mental Health and Chemical  Dependence\nInteragency  Task  Force  that  coordinates behavioral health and social\nsupports.\n \n  Actively participates in the State's Alzheimer's Disease  Coordinating\nCouncil,  which  has  produced  significant improvements in services, as\nwell as $25 million in funding to expand respite and other  supports  to\ncaregivers of individuals with dementia.\n \n  Actively  participates  in the State's Most Integrated Setting Coordi-\nnating Council (MISCC) which is charged with  implementing  the  State's\n \nOlmstead   Plan,   and  the  Governor's  Anti-Poverty  and  homelessness\nreduction initiatives.\n \n  In  sum,  producing  a  second duplicative report, as well as managing\nanother interagency council, will require additional sources of  funding\nthat  have  yet to be identified. For these reasons, I am constrained to\nveto this bill.\n \n  The bill is disapproved.                    (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n \n                               __________\n",
    "signedDate": null,
